= Identity drift (artificial intelligence) — Technical =

== Anchor-state formulation ==
Let <code>K</code> be a set of identity-relevant anchors and <code>Z_t</code> the agent's current identity-relevant state:

<pre>
K = {role, commitments, constraints, ownership, policy, ...}
Z_t = current represented/operational identity state
</pre>

Identity drift exists when <code>Z_t</code> diverges from the intended interpretation of <code>K</code> without an authorized identity-changing transition.

The distance function is domain-specific; there is no canonical scalar "identity drift score."

== Longitudinal character ==
A single mismatch can be configuration error or state corruption.

Drift emphasizes trajectory:

<pre>
Z_0 → Z_1 → ... → Z_t
</pre>

where locally small updates accumulate into a materially different state.

== Relation to goal drift ==
Goal drift can be modeled as divergence of a goal component <code>G_t</code> from an assigned objective <code>G*</code>.

Identity drift is broader when the persistent identity includes additional components:

<pre>
Z_t = (G_t, role_t, constraints_t, self-model_t, scope_t, ...)
</pre>

The generalization is conceptual, not a claim that all these variables share one metric.

== Change authorization ==
A robust system should distinguish:

* allowed adaptation;
* explicit migration;
* operator-approved reconfiguration;
* inferred update;
* accidental drift.

Versioned identity anchors and provenance can make this distinction auditable.

== Detection ==
Possible methods include:

* invariant checks;
* semantic comparison against canonical commitments;
* signed/versioned configuration comparison;
* behavioral regression tests;
* self-model calibration;
* provenance-aware diffing;
* checkpoint comparison;
* independent policy evaluation.

Behavior alone may be insufficient because two internal states can produce similar short-term outputs.

== Correction ==
A correction procedure can:

<pre>
detect divergence
→ identify changed components
→ trace provenance
→ classify authorized vs unauthorized change
→ reconcile / rollback / migrate
→ issue new checkpoint/version
</pre>

Blind rollback can itself be wrong if the agent intentionally evolved.

== Research boundary ==
Direct goal-drift and persona-drift research supplies evidence for longitudinal behavioral/goal instability. The extension to a broader persistent identity state is BITwiki synthesis and should retain that label.

